<p>Every article in this collection has used the same online shop, and each looked at one corner of it with everything else held still: a TTL for the product page, a price change racing a slow reader, a stampede on the trending panel, Redis taken away. A real team has to make all of those decisions before the cache goes live, and some of them turn out to decide others.</p>

<p>This article designs the shop's cache from its requirements, in the order the collection taught the decisions. Each decision gets the number that settles it for this shop and a link to the article that holds the full reasoning, so you can repeat the steps for your own system without re-reading everything.</p>

<p>Putting the decisions together shows two things no single article could. The obvious one-minute TTL for prices caps the shop's hit ratio at 42% however much memory it has, which forces a different way of keeping prices fresh. And each protection against failure covers a different incident, which the last figure lets you see by switching them off one at a time. The article ends with a checklist for reviewing any cache design.</p>

<h2 id="requirements">The requirements</h2>

<p>A design starts from numbers, not from Redis. These are the shop's, at the evening peak. They are illustrative, but each is the kind of number you can get from your own logs, your product owner and a load test.</p>

<div class="dc-table-wrap"><table class="dc-table">
  <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>At peak</th><th>Changes</th><th>May be out of date by</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Product details: name, description, images, price</td><td>1,000 product page views a second, over 2,000,000 products</td><td>About one price or description edit a second; sometimes a sale reprices thousands at once</td><td>Price: 1 minute. The rest: an hour</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stock level shown on the product page</td><td>Read with every product page</td><td>With every order: 20 a second</td><td>5 seconds</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trending panel on the home page</td><td>500 home page views a second</td><td>Recomputed by a 200 ms aggregate query</td><td>A minute or two</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Price and stock at checkout</td><td>20 orders a second</td><td>As above</td><td>Not at all</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sessions and baskets</td><td>Every page</td><td>Every click that changes them</td><td>Not at all; a crash or failover may lose the last second or so of changes</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<p>Three more facts complete the picture. A product's details are about 1.8 KB of JSON. Popularity is skewed in the way <ArticleRef slug="" hash="skew">the introduction</ArticleRef> described: a sample of a day's logs fits a Zipf-like curve with an exponent of about 0.9, so the most popular product is read about 30 times a second and the median product about once every two hours. The exponent matters, and the introduction's web proxy traces were flatter, so two of the figures below let you try 0.8. And a load test against a copy of the <Term kind="database">database</Term> shows it answering about 300 of these queries a second before its latency starts to climb.</p>

<h2 id="budget">The database decides how good the cache must be</h2>

<p>Start with the question <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="dependency">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> asked first: could the shop run without a cache? Every product page needs the details and the stock level, so without a cache the database would receive 2,000 queries a second for product pages alone, nearly seven times what it can answer. This is a <strong>capacity cache</strong>: the shop cannot sustain its peak without it. That one fact makes the failure plan later in this article compulsory rather than optional.</p>

<p>It also turns the database's capacity into a budget that every later decision spends. Do not budget all of it. Operating a cache suggested paging when misses push the database past about 70% of its capacity, because the remaining margin is what an incident consumes. I apply that line to the database's whole load, so the shop has 210 queries a second to spend. Checkout will read the database directly, for reasons in the next section, at three queries per order (price, stock, and writing the order), which takes 60 off the top. That leaves 150 queries a second for every <Term kind="miss" mark>miss</Term> on product pages.</p>

<p>From there it is the arithmetic of <ArticleRef slug="" hash="what-it-buys">the introduction</ArticleRef>. Product pages make 2,000 reads a second of Redis, details and stock together, and at most 150 may miss:</p>

<p class="formula">miss ratio ≤ 150 ÷ 2,000 = 7.5%, so hit ratio ≥ 92.5%</p>

<p>Every decision below either earns <Term kind="hit" mark>hits</Term> towards that 92.5% or spends queries from the 150. The 92.5% holds at today's traffic with checkout reading the database; change either and the need changes with it, as Figure 2 will show.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-cache">What to cache, and what to leave out</h2>

<p><strong>Product details</strong> are the obvious candidate: shared by every shopper, read far more often than they change, and expensive enough to assemble that a copy saves real work. Cache them as one JSON entry per product, holding what the page needs and nothing else, the default <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="formats">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> recommends.</p>

<p><strong>Stock</strong> goes in its own entry. It changes with every order, 20 times a second across the catalogue, while the rest of a product changes about once a second; inside the product entry, every sale would delete 1.8 KB of unchanged details. <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="choosing">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> gave the rule: a key deleted on every write hits about <em>k</em> ÷ (<em>k</em> + 1) of its reads, where <em>k</em> is reads per write, so data that changes at different speeds belongs in different entries. One <code>MGET</code> still fetches both.</p>

<p><strong>The trending panel</strong> is one entry, read 500 times a second. It needs special care against stampedes, which comes later.</p>

<p><strong>The price and stock read at checkout are not cached.</strong> A cached price can be stale, and at checkout a stale price is not an old page but a wrong charge. <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="ttl">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> put a stock count at checkout in the same place: it may not be out of date at all. The page the shopper decided on may still have shown an older price, so the basket keeps the price the page showed, and when the database's price differs, checkout shows the new one and asks the shopper to confirm before charging. Checkout's two reads are 40 of its 60 queries a second, more than a quarter of what is left for misses: a fixed cost of charging the right price.</p>

<p><strong>Sessions and baskets are not cache at all.</strong> They exist nowhere else, so they need persistence, a replica and a memory policy that refuses writes rather than evicting: the opposite of a cache's settings. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="choosing">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> and <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="maxmemory">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> both put them on a separate Redis.</p>

<p>One thing stays out of each entry. Product pages show prices in the shopper's currency, but the entry holds the price in pounds and the application converts it after the read. <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="identity">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> said everything that changes an answer belongs in the key; caching the input rather than the answer keeps the currency out of the key, so one entry serves every shopper.</p>

<h2 id="pattern-and-keys">Pattern and keys</h2>

<p>The pattern is cache-aside with delete on write, the default <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="choosing">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> recommends because each part fails safely. A product page sends one <code>MGET</code> for the details and the stock; whatever misses is loaded from the database and stored. A change commits to the database first, and the keys it affects are deleted afterwards.</p>

<p>The keys follow the convention from Cache keys and cached values: an application prefix, the kind of entry, a version, then the identity. They are built in one function, from typed inputs, so each answer has exactly one name.</p>

<pre><code>{`shop:product:v3:42      product details, JSON
shop:stock:v1:42        stock level, an integer
shop:trending:v1        the trending panel, JSON
shop:lock:trending:v1   the trending panel's rebuild lock`}</code></pre>

<p>Two smaller decisions from the same article apply. Old links and invented ids ask for products that do not exist, so a lookup the database positively answers with "no such product" stores a marker for 30 seconds, and ids that are not well formed are rejected before the cache. And the version in each name is how a change to the entry's shape is deployed, which returns later as a failure case.</p>

<h2 id="ttl">How long may a copy live?</h2>

<p>The requirements say a price on a product page may be a minute old, so the obvious design, and what most first designs do, is <code>SET … EX 60</code> on every product entry and nothing else: let copies <Term kind="expiry" mark>expire</Term>, and a page can never show a price more than a minute old. Before trusting it, work out what hit ratio it buys.</p>

<p><ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="ttl">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> derived it for one key. A key read at random <em>r</em> times a second, stored on a miss and kept for a TTL of <em>T</em> seconds, gets one miss and then about <em>rT</em> hits per lifetime:</p>

<p class="formula">hit ratio for one key = <em>rT</em> ÷ (1 + <em>rT</em>)</p>

<p>A key read about once per TTL, <em>rT</em> = 1, hits half its reads; one read much less often misses nearly every time. What that article could not show is what this means for a whole catalogue, which is mostly rarely read keys. Figure 1 draws the shop's two million products by popularity, with a line at one read per TTL.</p>

<PopularityFigure number={1} />

<p>With the TTL at one minute, only the 4,152 products to the left of the shaded area are read at least once a minute. Everything in the shaded area, 1,995,848 products, is read less often, and together those products get 59% of all reads. Most of those reads find the copy already expired. Adding up <em>rT</em> ÷ (1 + <em>rT</em>) over every product gives the first readout: a one-minute TTL caps the product hit ratio at 42%, and because the figure assumes unlimited memory, no amount of memory changes that.</p>

<p>Now drag the TTL to an hour. The dashed line drops and the shaded area shrinks, though by less than the logarithmic axis suggests: only 392,713 products, a fifth of the catalogue, clear the line, and the other four-fifths still get 19% of the reads. Only at six hours does every product clear it, and even then the hit ratio is 93%, because a product read about once per TTL still misses half the time. Switch the exponent to 0.8 and every number gets worse: a flatter curve puts more of the reads in the tail.</p>

<p>Stock is worse again. Its requirement is five seconds, which caps its hit ratio at 25%: 748 misses a second from stock alone, two and a half times the database's capacity.</p>

<p>So a TTL cannot be both the freshness mechanism and the hit-ratio mechanism for this catalogue. Freshness comes instead from deleting a product's keys when it changes, as cache-aside already does on write, so a page shows a new price as soon as the delete lands. The TTL is then free to be long, chosen for the hit ratio, as a backstop for deletes that never arrive. Its cost has a ceiling, because each key expires at most once per TTL. With the 24-hour TTL and ±10% jitter chosen below, the shortest lifetime is 21.6 hours, so two million product keys cause at most 2,000,000 ÷ 77,760 ≈ 26 expiry misses a second, and the stock keys as many again.</p>

<p>Memory then becomes the other limit. A cache that keeps copies for a day would like to hold almost the whole catalogue, and with less memory it evicts the least recently read keys. Figure 2 models that with Che's approximation, which <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="lru-or-lfu">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> used: an LRU cache keeps a key while it is read again within a characteristic time, which is the same for every key and is set by how much fits. Details and stock levels share one Redis and one <code>maxmemory</code>, and one <code>MGET</code> reads both, so they are kept or evicted together. Each key gets whichever cap is tighter, the TTL's or memory's, and the note under the bar says which one binds.</p>

<BudgetFigure number={2} />

<p>The figure starts where most first designs start: copies that expire after a minute, and 2 GB of memory. The bar runs off the axis at 1,386 queries a second, while only 0.07 GB of memory is in use, so memory is not what is missing. Drag the TTL up a stop at a time. The product segment shrinks, but the price readout breaks the one-minute target, and the stock segment does not move, because stock still expires after five seconds. No TTL on its own gets the bar under the capacity line.</p>

<p>Now switch to deletes on change and take the TTL to 24 hours. The product row says "limited by memory", and the bar, at 336, is over capacity. Raise <code>maxmemory</code> a stop at a time. At 3 GB the total is 199, just under the alert line; at 3.5 GB it is 147. At 4 GB the row says "limited by the TTL": memory has stopped being the limit, the total is 123, and 96.9% of page reads hit against the 92.5% the budget needs. Notice that 5 GB changes nothing.</p>

<p>Last, switch checkout to read the price from Redis. The bar loses 40 queries a second, and the hit readout's need falls to 90.5%, because checkout now takes only 20 of the 210. The readout below it shows what was bought with them: the price charged is only as fresh as the page's, normally about a second old, but a day old if a delete is lost. Switch it back, and leave the two "What if" switches for later.</p>

<p>The design takes deletes on change, a 24-hour TTL and <code>maxmemory 4gb</code>, which leaves 87 queries a second below the alert line. Four gigabytes is the smallest setting at which memory stops being the limit, so errors in the inputs, such as the popularity exponent, barely move the total. What made the decision was not a preference for long TTLs but the shape of the popularity curve, which you can measure from a day of logs before writing a line of cache code.</p>

<h2 id="freshness">Keeping copies fresh</h2>

<p>Making deletes the freshness mechanism changes what a lost delete costs: with a 24-hour backstop, an old price can stay on a page for a day rather than a minute. This departs from the rule in <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="choosing">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> to set every TTL to how long a wrong value may be shown, the one remedy that caps every failure, including ones nobody foresaw, such as a missed key family. Two things make it acceptable here: checkout confirms any price change with the shopper, so a stale page never becomes a wrong charge, and a background job samples cached prices against the database and alerts on any mismatch. The design also has to make deletes reliable, and three remedies from that article stop being optional.</p>

<p><strong>Deletes go through a transactional outbox.</strong> A change to a product writes a row to an outbox table in the same transaction, and a relay process reads the outbox and deletes the product's keys, so a crash between commit and delete delays the delete instead of losing it, the remedy for <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="lost-deletes">lost deletes</ArticleRef>. A failover <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="failover-loss">can lose recent writes, deletes included</ArticleRef>, so the relay keeps the last few minutes of deletes and sends them again when the Sentinels' <code>+switch-master</code> event announces a new primary. The window must reach back further than <code>down-after-milliseconds</code> plus the failover, because a primary cut off by a network partition keeps accepting deletes until it is replaced, and discards them when it rejoins as a replica. The relay adds a second or so between commit and delete, and it sends the stock deletes too, so that gap must stay inside the tightest target it serves: five seconds for stock, not the minute for prices.</p>

<p><strong>Fills take a <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="leases">lease</ArticleRef>.</strong> The stale-set race from <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="stale-refill">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> lets a slow reader store a price it read before a change, after the change's delete, and with a long TTL that stale copy stays for a day. The shop makes nearly two million writes a day, so even a rare race happens. On a miss, the reader writes a placeholder token with <code>SET … NX PX 2000</code> before reading the database, and stores the value with <code>SET … IFEQ</code>, which succeeds only if its token is still there. A delete in between removes the token, so the late fill is refused; I checked the whole sequence on Redis 8.10.3.</p>

<p>Leases add three rules to the read path. Another reader's <code>MGET</code> returns the placeholder, so a value that starts with <code>lease:</code> counts as a miss, and a reader that finds someone else's lease reads the database without filling. The fill passes <code>EX</code> with the jittered TTL, because <code>SET … IFEQ</code> without it stores a value that never expires. And fills read the primary database, because a lease cannot protect a read from a lagging copy. Before Redis 8.4, the conditional fill is a short Lua script, and the application's user then needs <code>EVALSHA</code> and <code>EVAL</code>.</p>

<p><strong>A merchant sees their own change.</strong> A delete that lands a second later is not soon enough for the person who edited the price, so the merchant's admin pages read the database, <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="read-your-writes">the simplest form of read-your-writes</ArticleRef>, which costs nothing because there are few merchants.</p>

<p>One kind of change needs planning rather than mechanism. A sale that reprices the 20,000 most popular products in one transaction deletes keys that carry about half of all reads. From the same model, that causes about 380 misses in the first second and 1,500 within five, against 87 queries a second of headroom below the alert line; the same sale over 20,000 random products causes about 370 misses spread over a minute. So the relay paces a bulk change's deletes to about 50 popular products a second, in a queue of its own so that stock deletes are never held up behind it. That spreads these 20,000 over about seven minutes, and it has a price: the last products in the queue show the old price for up to seven minutes, well past the one-minute target. The shop accepts that for a sale that must start at peak, because checkout confirms every price with the shopper, and schedules large repricings away from the peak where it can.</p>

<h2 id="stampedes">Stampedes and synchronised expiry</h2>

<p><ArticleRef slug="stampedes" hash="choosing">Cache stampedes and the thundering herd</ArticleRef> gave the test for whether a key can stampede: its reads a second times the time to rebuild it. A product is rebuilt with one query of about 5 ms. The most popular product is read about 30 times a second, so a miss on it overlaps with about 30 × 0.005 = 0.15 other reads: no stampede, and no defence beyond what follows. Even a product promoted on the home page during a sale, at a tenth of all product reads, scores 0.5.</p>

<p>The trending panel scores 500 × 0.2 = 100. Without protection, every expiry sends about a hundred copies of a 200 ms aggregate query to the database at once. The design takes that article's answer for this panel: serve the old copy while one request refreshes it. The key has a hard TTL of 10 minutes and a soft expiry of 60 seconds: it counts as stale once less than 9 minutes remain, which the reader sees by pipelining <code>PTTL</code> with its <code>GET</code>. The first request to see a stale copy takes a rebuild lock with <code>SET shop:lock:trending:v1 … NX PX 5000</code>, checks that the copy is still stale, runs the query with a 2-second statement timeout so it finishes inside the lock, stores the new value and releases the lock with <code>DELEX … IFEQ</code>. Every other request keeps serving the old copy, so the database sees one trending query a minute. After an empty restart there is no old copy: requests wait briefly for the lock holder's value, then show a default panel rather than going to the database.</p>

<p>Every product and stock TTL also gets <strong>jitter</strong>: 24 hours plus or minus 10%, drawn at random on each fill. Without it, keys filled together expire together: after a deploy or restart at the evening peak, the popular products would be refilled within a minute of each other and expire together at the next evening's peak. <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="jitter">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> showed how jitter spreads them; here it spreads keys filled in the same minute over nearly five hours.</p>

<h2 id="memory">Memory and eviction</h2>

<p>Figure 2 settled on a <code>maxmemory</code> of 4 GB. To turn that into a server, I measured what each entry costs on Redis 8.10.3. Twenty thousand product entries of about 1.8 KB, each with a 24-hour TTL, grew <code>used_memory</code> by 2,131 bytes an entry; twenty thousand stock entries took 98 bytes each, because for a small value the key, the TTL and Redis's bookkeeping are most of the cost (<ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="key-memory">How Redis works</ArticleRef> explains the overhead). That was macOS's allocator, not the jemalloc of a Linux build, so measure on your production build. At today's peak nothing is evicted: about 1.83 million products and their stock levels are present at any moment, using 3.8 GB, and the rest are products whose copy expired and has not been read since. The trending panel is one key of about 2.6 KB.</p>

<p><ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="capacity">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> listed what Redis uses beyond its data: fragmentation, client buffers, and the buffers that feed a replica. Persistence is off, but a replica's full synchronisation still forks the primary, as <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="replication">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> describes (on 8.10.3 with persistence off, connecting a replica raised <code>total_forks</code> to 1), and while it runs, every page the primary writes is copied. On a read-mostly cache whose only writes are fills, that copy is small, and an 8 GB server leaves room for it and everything else.</p>

<p>The eviction policy is <code>allkeys-lru</code>, with <code>maxmemory-samples</code> left at 5. Every key in this Redis is a cache entry with a TTL, so any of them may be evicted; sessions are elsewhere. At today's peak, memory does not bind, so the policy matters when traffic or the catalogue grows. To check that Figure 2's model describes Redis when memory does bind, I ran a scaled-down version: 100,000 products with the same skew, memory for about 30,000 of them, and 5,000 reads a second for four minutes. Redis's <code>allkeys-lru</code> hit 76.9% of reads; the approximation predicted 77.3%.</p>

<p><code>allkeys-lfu</code> hit 79.7% in the same test, but that test read its marginal keys every 50 seconds. When the shop's memory binds, its marginal products are read every few hours, far longer than the one-minute <code>lfu-decay-time</code>, and <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="lru-or-lfu">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> shows that this defeats LFU. Replay a day of real reads at their real pace before switching.</p>

<h2 id="clients">Talking to Redis</h2>

<p>The client settings follow <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="choosing">Redis clients</ArticleRef>. A product page makes one round trip, an <code>MGET</code> of the details and the stock; on a miss, the leases for both keys go in one pipeline and the fills in another. Connect and command timeouts are a few milliseconds, set from Redis's measured latency rather than the library's default. Each application instance's pool is sized from its request rate and the round trip, and all the pools added up stay well below <code>maxclients</code>.</p>

<p>The library's own retries are turned off: <code>retry=Retry(NoBackoff(), 0)</code> in redis-py, <code>MaxRetries: -1</code> in go-redis. <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="retries">The clients article</ArticleRef> found that both libraries, by default, re-send commands after a timeout, which turns this design's safe steps into unsafe ones: a retried <code>SET … NX</code> for a lease or the trending lock finds its own first attempt and is refused, so the client believes someone else holds it. With retries off, every timeout means "unknown", and each step decides for itself. A read that times out is a miss. A lease that times out counts as not taken: the reader goes to the database and does not fill. A trending lock that times out counts as held by someone else, so the request serves the stale copy, and the lock expires within 5 seconds if it was taken. A fill that times out needs nothing, because a repeated or late <code>SET … IFEQ</code> is refused. Only the relay retries, with backoff, because a <code>DEL</code> is safe to repeat.</p>

<h2 id="servers">One server or several</h2>

<p>Does the cache need sharding? <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys" hash="one-server">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> gave three reasons to split a cache across servers: memory, the main thread's load, and the network. None applies. The data fits in 4 GB; peak traffic is a few thousand commands a second against a single server's capacity of well over a hundred thousand; and the network carries about 1,000 × 2 KB + 500 × 2.6 KB ≈ 3.3 MB a second. A sale's hottest product at 100 reads a second is nothing for Redis; the hot key in this shop would be the database's problem, and the cache absorbs it. One primary is enough, and simpler to run.</p>

<p>Whether it needs a replica is a different question, answered by the shop being a capacity cache. If the only Redis restarts empty, every product page misses at once, and the next section shows that at this shop's size the refill takes hours. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="choosing">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> found a fast failover to a replica better than an empty restart for a database that cannot take a cold start. So the design has one replica with the primary's memory, three Sentinels on separate machines with <code>down-after-milliseconds</code> of a few seconds, clients that discover the primary through Sentinel, a deliberate <code>SENTINEL FAILOVER</code> before planned maintenance, and persistence off, with no automatic restart of a primary that has no persistence. The replica must also accept the application's users, which, as the section on access shows, is not automatic.</p>

<p>Sessions and baskets get their own Redis: a primary and replica with the append-only file at <code>appendfsync everysec</code>, <code>maxmemory-policy noeviction</code>, and alerts on memory, because there a full Redis must refuse writes rather than forget baskets. A crash can still lose about the last second of writes, and a failover can lose writes the replica had not yet received; <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="choosing">the replication article</ArticleRef> covers what <code>WAIT</code>, <code>WAITAOF</code> and <code>min-replicas-to-write</code> can and cannot add. The requirement accepts losing about a second of basket changes; a shop that could not would keep baskets in the database.</p>

<h2 id="failure">When things fail</h2>

<p>A replica shortens an outage; it does not remove it, and the shop can also empty its cache without any server failing, by deploying a new key version or running <code>FLUSHALL</code>. Because this is a capacity cache, <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="degrading">Operating a cache</ArticleRef> applies in full. A circuit breaker around calls to Redis makes a missing Redis cost an immediate miss rather than a timeout. A limit on the work the database holds at once protects it, sized by Little's law: 300 queries a second, each allowed at most 0.2 seconds waiting and running, is about 60 at a time. A request the limit refuses gets a degraded page, without its recommendations or with an honest "try again in a moment", rather than a timeout.</p>

<p>The shop adds one thing to that article's design. The limit gives checkout first claim: product-page misses are refused once the database holds 45 queries' worth of work, while checkout may use all 60. When the database is short of capacity, it should spend it on the requests that make money.</p>

<p>Figure 3 runs the design with those decisions as switches and the incidents as buttons. To run in your browser it holds 1/100 of the shop's catalogue, 20,000 products, as in Operating a cache, with checkouts added. A cache refills in proportion to the keys it must fill, so this one recovers in seconds where the shop takes an hour or more: read it for which protection covers which incident, and after each step I give the shop's own numbers, from a separate model of the full catalogue. Nothing expires while it runs, and a dashed mark on the charts shows each change of switch.</p>

<PlaygroundFigure number={3} />

<p><strong>Losing the primary.</strong> With the design as it stands, press <em>Lose the primary</em>. Notice the shaded band on both charts: for the 6 seconds of the failover, product pages that would have hit are refused, about 15% of them over the half minute, while the dashed checkout line stays at zero. Everything is back as soon as the replica is promoted, because it has the data. This is the one result that does not change with size: the shop loses the same 6 seconds.</p>

<p><strong>Without the limit.</strong> Set the database limit to none and press <em>Lose the primary</em> again. Notice the dashed checkout line: about 98% of checkouts fail over the half minute, although the cache was gone for only 6 seconds. The database is left with a queue of product queries nobody is waiting for, and each checkout waits behind them. This is the backlog from Operating a cache, and checkout pays for it. With one server as well as no limit, the shop never recovers at all: the stuck state that <ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="failures">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> first showed.</p>

<p><strong>One server.</strong> Press Reset, set the limit back to checkout first and Redis to one server, and press <em>Lose the primary</em>. Now Redis is down for 10 seconds and returns empty. Notice that product pages are refused for about 35 seconds in all while the database refills the cache, and that checkouts never fail. At the shop's size the same restart refuses two-thirds of product pages at first and still 13% after an hour: about 1.2 million pages over 2.3 hours, because every page needs two keys refilled from a database that can spare 240 queries a second. That is why the design has a replica, does not restart an empty primary automatically, and lets nobody run <code>FLUSHALL</code>.</p>

<p><strong>One limit for all.</strong> Press Reset, keep one server, set the limit to one limit for all and lose the server. Notice the dashed line again: about 81% of checkouts fail while the cache refills, because product pages now compete with them for the same 60. Checkout first is what keeps the shop taking orders through those hours.</p>

<p><strong>A new key version.</strong> Set Redis back to a primary and replica and the limit to checkout first, press Reset, and press <em>Deploy a new key version</em>. Every product key changes name, so the cache is cold for products although no server failed, and pages are refused for about 30 seconds. At the shop's size it is about 50 minutes and 400,000 pages, and a ramp, which helped a new cluster in <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="warming">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>, would have to last over an hour at peak to avoid it. Now set "After a new key version, a miss reads" to the old key first, and deploy again. Notice that nothing changes on either chart.</p>

<p>That last setting is the design's answer for deploys, the "read the old cache first" option from Operating a cache. A miss on <code>shop:product:v4:42</code> takes the usual lease, reads <code>shop:product:v3:42</code>, and if it is there, converts it to the new shape, stores it under the new name with <code>SET … IFEQ</code> and deletes the old one, so the database sees only the normal miss rate. Until a rollback is no longer possible, the relay deletes under both names, as <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="versions">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> warns. A rollback to the v3 code would find the popular products missing, a cold start of its own, and 4 GB has no room to keep both copies, so a rollback is planned like a deploy that cannot convert: away from the peak, with the limit covering the rest. When the new shape cannot be built from the old, a paced job fills the new keys off-peak before the switch, most popular products first, until they carry about 80% of reads.</p>

<p>Each switch matters for a different incident, and none of them replaces another. The replica shortens a server failure but does nothing for a deploy. Reading the old key protects a deploy but not a crash. The limit is the only protection that covers every incident, including ones nobody planned for, and checkout first decides who pays when it acts. The model leaves out a great deal, as its caption says, including retries, which would make every case without a limit worse.</p>

<h2 id="watching">Watching and securing it</h2>

<p>The alerts are the set from <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="alerts">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>, with the shop's numbers in them. Page when the database's total load passes 210 queries a second, the alert line in Figure 2, and on the circuit breaker staying open. Count hit ratios in the application per key family, products, stock and trending, because the server-wide <code>keyspace_hits</code> mixes them; a product hit ratio falling from about 98% is the early sign that memory is short or a deploy changed a key. Watch evictions, <code>used_memory</code> against <code>maxmemory</code>, connections against <code>maxclients</code>, and <code>master_link_status</code> on the replica.</p>

<p>The design adds two alerts of its own. Freshness now depends on the outbox relay, so page when the oldest delete it has not yet sent is more than about 3 seconds old: stock on a page may be 5 seconds out of date, and nothing else on the dashboard would show it. And the job that samples cached prices against the database alerts on any mismatch.</p>

<p>Access follows the same article's <ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="security">security section</ArticleRef>: Redis on a private network, the <code>default</code> user switched off, and a user for each client with only the keys and commands it needs. One detail matters for this design. Users made with <code>ACL SETUSER</code> exist only on the server that ran the command, so after a failover the application would find its user missing on the new primary and lose the cache as surely as if it had been emptied. So the users live in an ACL file, deployed identically to every server, named by the <code>aclfile</code> setting and changed by editing the file and running <code>ACL LOAD</code> on each:</p>

<pre><code>{`user default off
user shop-app on #… ~shop:* resetchannels -@all +@connection -@dangerous +mget +get +pttl +set +delex +unlink
user shop-relay on #… ~shop:product:* ~shop:stock:* resetchannels -@all +@connection -@dangerous +del +unlink
user shop-replica on #… +psync +replconf +ping
user shop-sentinel on #… resetchannels &__sentinel__:hello +multi +slaveof +ping +exec +subscribe +config|rewrite +role +publish +info +client|setname +client|kill +script|kill
user shop-admin on #… ~* &* +@all`}</code></pre>

<p>The application's user lists the commands the design uses rather than whole categories, because categories let in more than they suggest: <code>+@read</code> includes <code>SCAN</code>, which key patterns do not filter, so a user granted it can list every key. Both servers set <code>masteruser shop-replica</code> and <code>masterauth</code>, because after a failover the old primary rejoins as a replica and must authenticate too; the Sentinels use <code>sentinel auth-user</code> and <code>sentinel auth-pass</code>, with the rules the Redis ACL documentation gives. Only operators hold <code>shop-admin</code>'s password. I loaded this file into a primary and a replica on Redis 8.10.3: the users worked on both, the application was refused <code>SCAN</code>, <code>FLUSHALL</code>, <code>CONFIG</code> and keys outside its prefix, and a user added with <code>ACL SETUSER</code> never reached the replica. That test used <code>redis-cli</code>, so test the file with your own client library in Sentinel mode too: some Sentinel-aware clients send <code>ROLE</code> or <code>INFO</code> after connecting, and this user is refused both.</p>

<h2 id="design">The design on one page</h2>

