  <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>The shop's answer</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Does it need a cache?</td><td>Yes; a capacity cache</td><td>2,000 product reads a second against a database that answers 300</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hit ratio needed</td><td>92.5% of product-page reads, details and stock, at today's peak</td><td>150 misses a second left after the 70% alert line and checkout; it rises with traffic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>What to cache</td><td>Product details; stock as its own key; the trending panel</td><td>Shared, read far more than changed; stock changes 20 times as often</td></tr>
    <tr><td>What not to cache</td><td>Price and stock at checkout, which asks the shopper to confirm any price change; sessions and baskets</td><td>A stale price is a wrong charge; baskets are data, not copies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Cache-aside, delete on write</td><td>Each part fails safely</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Keys</td><td><code>shop:product:v3:{'{id}'}</code>, <code>shop:stock:v1:{'{id}'}</code>, <code>shop:trending:v1</code>; currency applied after the read</td><td>One name per answer; one entry serves every shopper</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Outbox relay deletes, replayed after a failover; bulk changes paced in their own queue; leases on fills; merchants read the database; cached prices sampled against the database</td><td>No TTL short enough for the price target gives enough hits</td></tr>
    <tr><td>TTL</td><td>24 hours ± 10% on products and stock</td><td>A backstop chosen for the hit ratio; jitter so keys filled together do not expire together</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stampedes</td><td>Trending: serve stale, soft 60 s, hard 10 min, rebuild lock; products: nothing more</td><td>Reads × rebuild time: 100 for trending, 0.15 for the busiest product</td></tr>
