<h2 id="what-would-change">What would change the design</h2>

<p>Every number in that table came from a requirement, so it is worth knowing which requirements it is most sensitive to. Go back to Figure 2, set the design (deletes on change, 24 hours, 4 GB, checkout from the database) and use the two "What if" switches.</p>

<p><strong>Less skew.</strong> Set the popularity exponent to 0.8. The total barely moves, from 123 to 124 queries a second, because at 4 GB memory is not the limit. Drop <code>maxmemory</code> to 3.5 GB and it rises to 181, with only 29 to spare. Below the point where memory stops limiting, the exponent matters a great deal, which is why the design sits at that point rather than just below it.</p>

<p><strong>Twice the traffic.</strong> Set the exponent back to 0.9 and the traffic to twice the peak. The total is 204, just under the alert line. Notice the hit readout: the need has risen to 97.8%, because checkout's queries doubled to 120 and what is left must cover misses from twice the reads. At 4 GB the cache just meets it, at 97.9%; at 3.5 GB it does not, and the total is 284. Checkout's 120 is the one part more memory cannot reduce, because it was deliberately left out of the cache. Beyond that, the answer is a bigger database, or moving reads that tolerate lag, such as merchants' reports, to database replicas. Checkout itself keeps reading the primary, because an asynchronous replica would bring back the staleness that keeping it out of the cache avoided.</p>

<p><strong>Listing pages</strong>, with 24 products each, are the natural next exercise. The same steps apply, with one new question: whether a listing entry holds product ids, fetched with one <code>MGET</code>, or whole product cards, which brings the fan-out problem from <ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="fan-out">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef>.</p>

<p><strong>Several regions</strong>, each with its own Redis and a read-only copy of the database, add a new race: a delete can reach a region before that region's copy of the database has the change, and a miss there then caches the old value. Facebook's memcache paper describes the design that avoids it, which is beyond this collection.</p>

<h2 id="checklist">A design review checklist</h2>

<p>These are the questions I would ask of any cache design, including this one, each with its reason and the place in the collection that answers it.</p>

<ol class="dc-checklist">
  <li><strong>If the cache vanished now, what would the database see, and could it take it?</strong> It decides whether the failure plan is optional. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="dependency">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>What does the database leave for misses, after the alert margin and everything not cached?</strong> That sets the hit ratio, and it moves with traffic. (<ArticleRef slug="" hash="what-it-buys">The introduction</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>For each kind of data, how stale may it be, and what happens if it is staler?</strong> A price being charged is read from the database, and the shopper confirms any change from the page. (<ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="choosing">Caching patterns</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Can a TTL that meets the staleness target also meet the hit ratio, given how often each key is read?</strong> If not, freshness must come from deletes. (<ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="ttl">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>What happens to a delete that is lost, and to a fill that races one?</strong> The TTL bounds both, so a long one needs an outbox and leases. (<ArticleRef slug="invalidation" hash="choosing">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Does every input that changes an answer appear in its key, and nothing else?</strong> A missing input serves one user's answer to another. (<ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="identity">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Which keys have reads a second times rebuild time well above 1?</strong> Only those need stampede protection. (<ArticleRef slug="stampedes" hash="choosing">Cache stampedes</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>How much memory does the target hit ratio need, in bytes per entry, and what is left for buffers and a replica's fork?</strong> Value sizes alone underestimate small entries badly. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="capacity">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Which eviction policy, and was it judged at the real request rate?</strong> A fast replay hides the LRU clock and LFU's decay. (<ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="lru-or-lfu">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Is anything in this Redis not a cache?</strong> If so, it needs its own Redis, with persistence and <code>noeviction</code>. (<ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="choosing">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Is any single value, key or command big or hot enough to hold up everyone else?</strong> One thread serves every client. (<ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="slow-command">How Redis works</ArticleRef>, <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys" hash="hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>How many round trips does a page make, and what does the client library re-send after a timeout?</strong> Retries turn a slow Redis into an overloaded one, and a lock into a false refusal. (<ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="retries">Redis clients</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Is a replica cheaper than an empty restart, at the cache's real size?</strong> Refill time grows with the number of keys. (<ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence" hash="choosing">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>What limits the work sent to the database when the cache is gone, and who is served first?</strong> Without a limit, a cold cache can stay cold. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="limit">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>How does a new key version, a new cluster or a flush reach traffic?</strong> Each is a cold start unless the old cache is read first or the new one is warmed. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="warming">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Which alert fires first when the hit ratio falls, and has the path for a missing cache been tested?</strong> An untested fallback usually fails when first needed. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="testing">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
  <li><strong>Who can run <code>FLUSHALL</code>, <code>SCAN</code> or <code>CONFIG</code>, and does every server, replicas included, have the same users?</strong> Users are not replicated. (<ArticleRef slug="operating-a-cache" hash="security">Operating a cache</ArticleRef>)</li>
</ol>

<p>That is the whole collection in one design: find whether a system needs a cache and what hit ratio its database demands, check whether a TTL can deliver it or freshness must come from deletes, size memory from measured bytes per entry, choose the topology from what an empty restart costs at real size, and give each incident its protection. The habit that ties it together is to write down what the database can answer before anything else, and to check each decision against that number.</p>

